A solid colour is not a pattern, and nil is the answer that says so.

colorUsingColorSpaceName: NSPatternColorSpace is the ONLY way a caller can ask whether a colour is
a pattern, so nil there is an answer and not a failure. This tree got that backwards in two
independent places, and both had to go before the symptom moved.

ONE: the two argument form MANUFACTURED a pattern, in four copies, one per source colour space. It
built a blank 1x1 NSImage, wrapped it in a CGPattern, and returned a colour claiming to be in
NSPatternColorSpace. Those four blocks are where the twelve 1x1 offscreen windows in every Qt run
came from; the count is 0 now.

TWO: the one argument form is a SEPARATE implementation, and it returned self for any space it
could not produce, with a comment naming patterns as one of them and arguing that self was better
than nil. Qt calls this one. Fixing only the two argument form changed the 1x1 windows and did not
move the crash at all, which is what said there were two entry points.

What Qt does with a non nil answer, from its own disassembly:

    NSColor *p = [color colorUsingColorSpaceName: NSPatternColorSpace];
    if (p) {
        NSImage *img = [p patternImage];
        qtBrush.setTexture(qt_mac_toQPixmap(img, QSizeF([img size].width, [img size].height)));
    }

patternImage is nil for a colour that is not really a pattern, [nil size] is 0x0, and QPixmap(0,0)
has a null handle. QMacCGContext::initialize then reads 0x24 off that null handle and dies. Stack,
measured from the core: QApplicationPrivate::init, initializeWidgetPalettesFromTheme, libqcocoa,
qt_mac_toQBrush, qt_mac_toQPixmap, QMacCGContext::initialize.

CMake.app now gets past its whole palette. The fault moves to CFDictionaryContainsKey with a NULL
dictionary, under CoreText and the Qt cocoa FONT code, which is a different and later defect.
Task #227.
